  2394	def _reviewed_test_command(root, prior):
  2395	    """(command, readable) — the test command as it stood at the last point the
  2396	    evidence chain actually covered.
  2397	
  2398	    Comparing only the previous version (`rev-list -2`) could be laundered: set
  2399	    `test_command` to `true` in one commit, touch any other config field in the
  2400	    next, and both observed versions read `true`. So the comparison point is the
  2401	    validated baseline `prior` — and when nothing has ever validated (the
  2402	    baseline is the bare root, which is also what a tampered command produces,
  2403	    since rewriting it invalidates the ancestor evidence), the config's FIRST
  2404	    version, because the whole history is then the span being judged.
  2405	
  2406	    A config with only one commit touching it was created, not changed: that is
  2407	    adoption, and a floor that fires on adoption is a floor people route around.
  2408	    `readable` is False only when git could not answer, which fails closed.
  2409	    """
