     1	# Protocol changelog
     2	
     3	Operational history of the Coverloop Multi-Model Production Protocol. The live doc (`CLAUDE.md`) carries only the current version; the story lives here.
     4	
     5	## 2026-09-04 — one command at the boundary (PROTOCOL v2.11.0)
     6	
     7	An evidence-driven review of the fleet's own logs asked whether Coverloop had
     8	become too heavy to follow. It had, in four specific ways — each fixed here,
     9	and none by weakening a gate.
    10	
    11	- **The tier is derived, not declared.** `attest` no longer takes the tier from
    12	  the caller: it classifies the same paths `gate` would, with the same floor
    13	  semantics, and records `tier_source`, the computed `floor` and its reasons.
    14	  Below the floor is refused (exit 2). `--raise-tier <T> --reason "<why>"`
    15	  elevates **upward only** and writes the reason into the report, for risk the
    16	  path classifier cannot see (a milestone gate covering many migrations, a
    17	  config file that steers auth). The legacy `--tier` pin still works above the
    18	  floor — recorded as an elevation with an explicit "no reason given" marker,
    19	  plus a migration note on stderr — so every pre-2.11 caller, including the four
    20	  SHA-pinned CI workflows, keeps passing unchanged.
    21	
    22	- **Verification is not authorization.** The gate's baseline walk used to demand
    23	  a human signature before it would trust an ancestor's evidence. In the fleet
    24	  that dropped 238 fully reviewed L3 commits from the baseline: their files fell
    25	  back into every later change's floor, so docs-only commits inherited L3 from
    26	  the repo root and the gate's floors were systematically inflated. The walk now
    27	  asks the *verification* question — tier covers the segment, tests passed,
    28	  required reviews passed — via an explicit `EVIDENCE_VERIFICATION` scope. The
    29	  human gate is unchanged where it decides anything: `gate` and `check` still
    30	  fail on it at HEAD, and an ancestor with missing or failing tests or reviews
    31	  still stops the baseline dead (both directions are regression-locked).
    32	
    33	- **`coverloop check` — one command at the merge boundary.** Refuse a dirty tree
    34	  → derive the tier → run the tests → run the reviews that tier requires and
    35	  capture their transcripts → bind to HEAD → run the real `gate`. It prints
    36	  `SAFE TO MERGE` or exactly **one** `STOP: <reason>`, and it is a wrapper over
    37	  the existing primitives with no new authority: `classify`, `attest` and `gate`
    38	  are untouched and still what CI runs. The reviewer lineage is configuration,
    39	  not architecture — `"reviewers": {"primary": "codex", "secondary": "glm"}`
    40	  with `"reviewer_commands"` — so Codex is a default, not an assumption. A
    41	  missing reviewer command is a STOP that names the role; a transcript that does
    42	  not end in an unambiguous pass marker is a `fail`, persisted as one.
    43	
    44	- **The session hook is now seven lines of invariants.** The SessionStart hook
    45	  ran on every session and after every compaction, and at 6 KB it turned the
    46	  builder into a process manager: classify every task, pick a model, run two
    47	  reviewers, re-attest, keep a ledger, end every reply with a model line —
    48	  measured at **1% compliance** for the last of those. It now says: build
    49	  normally, run the relevant tests, run `coverloop check` before merge/deploy,
    50	  never lower the floor, never self-approve, never bypass red, never send
    51	  secrets or PII. Enforcement lives in code, where it is fail-closed; prose is
    52	  reserved for what no command can enforce.
    53	
    54	This release was itself elevated to **L3**. The path classifier reads it as L2,
    55	but the diff changes the attestation authority itself — baseline validation,
    56	reviewer execution, verdict parsing, and what `SAFE TO MERGE` means — which is
    57	exactly the intent-level risk `--raise-tier --reason` exists for.
    58	
    59	Eight cold review rounds ran against this change; the gate stopped its own merge
    60	every time. 39 findings, all verified against the code, and the pattern in them
    61	decided the shape of the release:
    62	
    63	**Item 2 — "verification is not authorization" — is DEFERRED, not shipped.**
    64	Letting a reviewed-but-uncountersigned commit advance the gate's baseline needs
    65	an obligation to be carried forward instead, and that mechanism produced a
    66	fail-open in **every one of the five rounds**: shed by stacking a commit on top
    67	(round 1), shed under `--human-gate-scope irreversible` (round 2), shed by an L0
    68	successor and by `scope` going retroactively sticky (round 3), lost through
    69	`--base` (round 4), and finally carrying only the human approval while dropping
    70	the reviewer requirement entirely (round 5). Each fix created the next finding.
    71	The baseline therefore stays exactly as released: it advances only under the
    72	full authorization requirements. The fleet's inflated floors remain — that is
    73	over-strictness, which is the safe direction to be wrong in — and item 2 returns
    74	later as an isolated design problem with this history attached.
    75	
    76	What the rounds did close, in what did ship:
    77	
    78	- **The reviewer is named outside the repo, and runs outside it.** Roles and
    79	  commands come from `~/.config/coverloop/reviewers.json` (or
    80	  `$COVERLOOP_REVIEWERS`), never the worktree; `check` refuses a policy that
    81	  resolves inside the repo under review (symlinks included), a
    82	  group/world-writable one, and a `.coverloop/config.json` that defines
    83	  reviewers at all. Token checks alone could not hold — `python3 -m reviewer`
    84	  picks up a planted `reviewer.py`, `sh <scripts/reviewer` hides the path in a
    85	  redirect token — so the command now executes from a scratch directory with
    86	  `PYTHONPATH` dropped and every repo-reachable `PATH` entry filtered out. What
    87	  it needs arrives explicitly: `$COVERLOOP_REPO`, `$COVERLOOP_DIFF`,
    88	  `$COVERLOOP_BASE`. The packet covers the same span the floor was computed
    89	  over, a `git` failure while producing it stops the run rather than handing a
    90	  reviewer an empty diff, and the scratch copy is cleaned up on every path.
    91	- **The verdict is a machine protocol.** Exactly `VERDICT: PASS`, as the
    92	  reviewer's closing word (only a CLI footer may follow), with no other
    93	  `VERDICT:` line anywhere in the transcript — matched case-insensitively so a
    94	  lowercase `verdict: fail` is caught and rejected rather than skipped.
    95	  `PASS WITH RISKS`, `APPROVE`, `LGTM`, a truncated log and silence all fail.
    96	- **`check` holds its own boundary.** It refuses a dirty tree, re-checks the
    97	  tree and HEAD after the tests and reviewers have run (a test that repairs the
    98	  code it tests must not earn `SAFE TO MERGE` for the broken commit being
    99	  shipped), honours attest's exit status so evidence git would ignore stops the
   100	  run, reports exactly one `STOP:` and exit 1 — and in `--json` owns file
   101	  descriptor 1, not just Python's `sys.stdout`, since the test command and the
   102	  reviewers are subprocesses that inherit it.
   103	- **An elevation cannot claim the classifier's exemption.** Under
   104	  `--human-gate-scope irreversible` the exemption reads the classified paths,
   105	  and `--raise-tier` exists precisely because the classifier under-reads the
   106	  change. An elevation's recorded reason also survives the ordinary
   107	  `check` -> `attest --approve` flow instead of being folded back into
   108	  "derived".
   109	- **CI was running 102 of 264 tests.** `unittest.main()` sat above classes
   110	  appended later in `tests/test_properties.py`, and CI invokes that file
