commit ff2f216fde771b08b8fbe10adf0171b487922eb2
Author:     Daniel Siman Tov <[REDACTED:email]>
AuthorDate: Fri Sep 4 12:45:36 2026 +0300
Commit:     Daniel Siman Tov <[REDACTED:email]>
CommitDate: Fri Sep 4 12:45:36 2026 +0300

    close the four findings from the eighth cold review
    
    The first round where both reviewers actually ran. GLM returned PASS and
    correctly called three things false positives; it also caught a comment in
    _validated_baseline still describing item 2's deferred intent as shipped
    behaviour, which is now the honest record of what ships and what does not.
    
    Codex returned FAIL with four:
    
    - P1 A second config commit laundered a weakened test command: set it to `true`
      in one commit, touch any other config field in the next, and comparing the
      last two versions sees `true` in both. The comparison point is now the
      validated baseline, falling back to the config's FIRST version when nothing
      has ever validated — which is exactly the state a tampered command produces,
      since rewriting it invalidates the ancestor evidence.
    - P1 The configured test command runs first, with the operator's privileges,
      and can overwrite a user-owned reviewer wrapper without touching a repository
      file. check now fingerprints each reviewer's resolved executable before and
      after the tests and stops on a change. An arbitrary test command is still
      arbitrary code: a gate is not a sandbox, and the limit is documented.
    - P1 An approval given at a lower tier survived a later elevation, so an L1
      sign-off could authorize an L3 whose reason did not exist yet. Raising an
      existing report's tier clears the approval and says so on stderr.
    - P2 Multiple VERDICT: PASS markers passed although the protocol says no other
      VERDICT: line may appear. Exactly one is required now.
    
    274 via discovery, 148 via the CI entry point, 112 in the gate suite.

diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
